Let $A = (2,70^\circ),$ $B = (2 \sqrt{3}, 40^\circ),$ and $C = (3,10^\circ).$
In rectangular coordinates,
\begin{align*} A &= (2 \cos 70^\circ, 2 \sin 70^\circ) \\ B &= (2 \sqrt{3} \cos 40^\circ, 2 \sqrt{3} \sin 40^\circ) \\ C &= (3 \cos 10^\circ, 3 \sin 10^\circ) \end{align*}
The area of triangle $ABC$ is given by
\begin{align*} \frac{1}{2} |(x_A - x_C)(y_B - y_A) - (x_A - x_B)(y_C - y_A)| &= \frac{1}{2} |(2 \cos 70^\circ - 3 \cos 10^\circ)(2 \sqrt{3} \sin 40^\circ - 2 \sin 70^\circ) \\ &\quad - (2 \cos 70^\circ - 2 \sqrt{3} \cos 40^\circ)(3 \sin 10^\circ - 2 \sin 70^\circ)| \end{align*}
We can also use the formula for the area of a triangle in polar coordinates:
\[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} |r_1 r_2 \sin (\theta_2 - \theta_1) + r_2 r_3 \sin (\theta_3 - \theta_2) + r_3 r_1 \sin (\theta_1 - \theta_3)| \]
In this case, $r_1 = 2,$ $\theta_1 = 70^\circ,$ $r_2 = 2 \sqrt{3},$ $\theta_2 = 40^\circ,$ and $r_3 = 3,$ $\theta_3 = 10^\circ.$  Then
\begin{align*} \text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} |2(2 \sqrt{3}) \sin (40^\circ - 70^\circ) + 2 \sqrt{3}(3) \sin (10^\circ - 40^\circ) + 3(2) \sin (70^\circ - 10^\circ)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |4 \sqrt{3} \sin (-30^\circ) + 6 \sqrt{3} \sin (-30^\circ) + 6 \sin 60^\circ| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |4 \sqrt{3} \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) + 6 \sqrt{3} \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) + 6 \left( \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right)| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |-2 \sqrt{3} - 3 \sqrt{3} + 3 \sqrt{3}| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} |-2 \sqrt{3}| \\ &= \frac{1}{2} (2 \sqrt{3}) \\ &= \sqrt{3} \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\sqrt{3}}$

