We can use the sum-to-product formulas
\begin{align*} \sin x + \sin y &= 2 \sin \frac{x+y}{2} \cos \frac{x-y}{2} \\ \cos x + \cos y &= 2 \cos \frac{x+y}{2} \cos \frac{x-y}{2} \end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*} \frac{\sin{10^\circ}+\sin{20^\circ}}{\cos{10^\circ}+\cos{20^\circ}} &= \frac{2 \sin \frac{10^\circ+20^\circ}{2} \cos \frac{10^\circ-20^\circ}{2}}{2 \cos \frac{10^\circ+20^\circ}{2} \cos \frac{10^\circ-20^\circ}{2}} \\ &= \frac{2 \sin 15^\circ \cos (-5^\circ)}{2 \cos 15^\circ \cos (-5^\circ)} \\ &= \frac{\sin 15^\circ}{\cos 15^\circ} \\ &= \tan 15^\circ. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\tan 15}$

