Let the line be given by $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 - 2t \\ 6t \\ 1 - 3t \end{pmatrix}.$  Let $P = (2,3,4).$  Then the vector from the point $(4 - 2t, 6t, 1 - 3t)$ to $(2,3,4)$ is
\begin{align*} \begin{pmatrix} 2 - (4 - 2t) \\ 3 - 6t \\ 4 - (1 - 3t) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2t - 2 \\ 3 - 6t \\ 3 + 3t \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}We want this vector to be orthogonal to $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix},$ so
\begin{align*} \begin{pmatrix} 2t - 2 \\ 3 - 6t \\ 3 + 3t \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} &= 0 \\ -2(2t - 2) + 6(3 - 6t) - 3(3 + 3t) &= 0 \\ -4t + 4 + 18 - 36t - 9 - 9t &= 0 \\ -49t + 13 &= 0 \\ 49t &= 13 \\ t &= \frac{13}{49} \end{align*}Then
\begin{align*} \begin{pmatrix} 4 - 2t \\ 6t \\ 1 - 3t \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} 4 - 2 \cdot \frac{13}{49} \\ 6 \cdot \frac{13}{49} \\ 1 - 3 \cdot \frac{13}{49} \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 4 - \frac{26}{49} \\ \frac{78}{49} \\ 1 - \frac{39}{49} \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} \frac{196 - 26}{49} \\ \frac{78}{49} \\ \frac{49 - 39}{49} \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} \frac{170}{49} \\ \frac{78}{49} \\ \frac{10}{49} \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\left( \frac{170}{49}, \frac{78}{49}, \frac{10}{49} \right)}$

