\boxed{2 \tan x}
We have
\begin{align*} \frac{\cos x}{1 - \sin x} - \frac{\cos x}{1 + \sin x} &= \frac{\cos x (1 + \sin x) - \cos x (1 - \sin x)}{(1 - \sin x)(1 + \sin x)} \\ &= \frac{\cos x + \cos x \sin x - \cos x + \cos x \sin x}{1 - \sin^2 x} \\ &= \frac{2 \cos x \sin x}{\cos^2 x} \\ &= \frac{2 \sin x}{\cos x} \\ &= 2 \tan x. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{2 \tan x}$

