Let the equations of the planes be
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} -x + cy + bz &= 0 \\ cx - y + az &= 0 \\ bx + ay - z &= 0 \end{align*}
Since the three planes contain the same line, the system of equations
\begin{align*} -x + cy + bz &= 0 \\ cx - y + az &= 0 \\ bx + ay - z &= 0 \end{align*}
must have infinitely many solutions.  This means that the determinant of the coefficient matrix must be 0:
\begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} -1 & c & b \\ c & -1 & a \\ b & a & -1 \end{vmatrix} &= -1 \begin{vmatrix} -1 & a \\ a & -1 \end{vmatrix} - c \begin{vmatrix} c & a \\ b & -1 \end{vmatrix} + b \begin{vmatrix} c & -1 \\ b & a \end{vmatrix} \\ &= -1(1 - a^2) - c(-c - ab) + b(ac + b) \\ &= -1 + a^2 + c^2 + abc + abc + b^2 \\ &= a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + 2abc - 1 = 0 \end{align*}
Thus, $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + 2abc = 1.$

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{1}$

