We are asked to add the numbers $1_2 + 11_2 + 111_2 + 1111_2$. We can write these numbers in base 10 as $1$, $3$, $7$, and $15$. Then $1+3+7+15 = 26$.
Now we convert 26 to base 2.
$26 \div 2 = 13 R 0$
$13 \div 2 = 6 R 1$
$6 \div 2 = 3 R 0$
$3 \div 2 = 1 R 1$
$1 \div 2 = 0 R 1$
Reading the remainders from bottom to top, we have $11010_2$.

Alternatively, we can add in base 2:
\begin{align*} & \quad 1 \\ & \quad 11 \\ & \quad 111 \\ + & \quad 1111 \\ \hline \end{align*}
Starting from the rightmost column, $1+1+1+1 = 4 = 100_2$, so we write down 0 and carry-over 1.
\begin{align*} & \quad \stackrel{1}{1} \\ & \quad 11 \\ & \quad 111 \\ + & \quad 1111 \\ \hline & \quad 0 \end{align*}
Next column, $1+1+1+1 = 4 = 100_2$, so we write down 0 and carry-over 1.
\begin{align*} & \quad \stackrel{1}{1} \stackrel{1}{1} \\ & \quad 11 \\ & \quad 111 \\ + & \quad 1111 \\ \hline & \quad 00 \end{align*}
Next column, $1+1+1+1 = 4 = 100_2$, so we write down 0 and carry-over 1.
\begin{align*} & \quad \stackrel{1}{1} \stackrel{1}{1} \stackrel{1}{1} \\ & \quad 11 \\ & \quad 111 \\ + & \quad 1111 \\ \hline & \quad 000 \end{align*}
Next column, $1+1+1+1 = 4 = 100_2$, so we write down 0 and carry-over 1.
\begin{align*} & \quad \stackrel{1}{1} \stackrel{1}{1} \stackrel{1}{1} \stackrel{1}{1} \\ & \quad 11 \\ & \quad 111 \\ + & \quad 1111 \\ \hline & \quad 0000 \end{align*}
Finally, we write down the carry-over 1 and the carry-over 1, so we have $11010_2$.

\boxed{11010_2}

