\]Find the smallest possible value for $a$ if the graph of $y=f(x)$ is continuous (which means the graph can be drawn without lifting your pencil from the paper).

output:
}
Let the given expression be
\begin{align*} \cos^2 x + \cos^2 \left( \frac{\pi}{3} + x \right) + \cos^2 \left( \frac{\pi}{3} - x \right) &= \cos^2 x + \frac{1 + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{3} + 2x \right)}{2} + \frac{1 + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{3} - 2x \right)}{2} \\ &= \cos^2 x + \frac{1 + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{3} + 2x \right) + 1 + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{3} - 2x \right)}{2} \\ &= \cos^2 x + \frac{2 + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{3} + 2x \right) + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{3} - 2x \right)}{2} \\ &= \cos^2 x + 1 + \frac{1}{2} \left[ \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{3} + 2x \right) + \cos \left( \frac{2 \pi}{3} - 2x \right) \right] \\ &= \cos^2 x + 1 + \frac{1}{2} \left[ 2 \cos \frac{2 \pi}{3} \cos 2x \right] \\ &= \cos^2 x + 1 + \cos \frac{2 \pi}{3} \cos 2x \\ &= \cos^2 x + 1 + \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) \cos 2x \\ &= \cos^2 x + 1 - \frac{1}{2} (2 \cos^2 x - 1) \\ &= \cos^2 x + 1 - \cos^2 x + \frac{1}{2} \\ &= 1 + \frac{1}{2} \\ &= \frac{3}{2}. \end{align*}
Thus, the given expression is equal to $\frac{3}{2}.$

