We are asked to compute $2911_{11} - 1392_{11}$ in base 11.
We have
\begin{align*} 2911_{11} &= 2 \cdot 11^3 + 9 \cdot 11^2 + 1 \cdot 11^1 + 1 \cdot 11^0 \\ &= 2 \cdot 1331 + 9 \cdot 121 + 1 \cdot 11 + 1 \cdot 1 \\ &= 2662 + 1089 + 11 + 1 \\ &= 3763 \end{align*}
and
\begin{align*} 1392_{11} &= 1 \cdot 11^3 + 3 \cdot 11^2 + 9 \cdot 11^1 + 2 \cdot 11^0 \\ &= 1 \cdot 1331 + 3 \cdot 121 + 9 \cdot 11 + 2 \cdot 1 \\ &= 1331 + 363 + 99 + 2 \\ &= 1795 \end{align*}
Thus, $2911_{11} - 1392_{11} = 3763 - 1795 = 1968$.
Now we convert 1968 to base 11.
\begin{align*} 1968 \div 11 &= 178 \text{ R } 10 \\ 178 \div 11 &= 16 \text{ R } 2 \\ 16 \div 11 &= 1 \text{ R } 5 \\ 1 \div 11 &= 0 \text{ R } 1 \end{align*}
The remainders, read from bottom to top, are 1, 5, 2, 10. Since 10 is represented by A in base 11, we have $1968 = 152A_{11}$.

Alternatively, we can subtract directly in base 11:
\begin{array}{@{}c@{\,}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} & 2 & 9 & 1 & 1_{11} \\ - & 1 & 3 & 9 & 2_{11} \\ \hline \end{array}
We start with the rightmost column: $1 - 2$. We need to borrow from the next column. The 1 in the $11^1$ place becomes 0, and the 1 in the $11^0$ place becomes $1+11 = 12$. Then $12 - 2 = 10$, which is A in base 11.
\begin{array}{@{}c@{\,}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} & 2 & 9 & 0 & 12_{11} \\ - & 1 & 3 & 9 & 2_{11} \\ \hline & & & & A_{11} \end{array}
Next, we have $0 - 9$. We need to borrow from the next column. The 9 in the $11^2$ place becomes 8, and the 0 in the $11^1$ place becomes $0+11 = 11$. Then $11 - 9 = 2$.
\begin{array}{@{}c@{\,}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} & 2 & 8 & 11 & 12_{11} \\ - & 1 & 3 & 9 & 2_{11} \\ \hline & & & 2 & A_{11} \end{array}
Next, we have $8 - 3 = 5$.
\begin{array}{@{}c@{\,}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} & 2 & 8 & 11 & 12_{11} \\ - & 1 & 3 & 9 & 2_{11} \\ \hline & & 5 & 2 & A_{11} \end{array}
Finally, we have $2 - 1 = 1$.
\begin{array}{@{}c@{\,}c@{}c@{}c@{}c} & 2 & 8 & 11 & 12_{11} \\ - & 1 & 3 & 9 & 2_{11} \\ \hline & 1 & 5 & 2 & A_{11} \end{array}
Thus, $2911_{11} - 1392_{11} = 152A_{11}$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{152A_{11}}$

