Let the direction vector of the first line be $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix},$ and the direction vector of the second line be $\mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}.$
Then
\begin{align*} \cos \theta &= \frac{|\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{w}|}{\|\mathbf{v}\| \|\mathbf{w}\|} \\ &= \frac{\left| \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \right|}{\left\| \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \right\| \left\| \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} \right\|} \\ &= \frac{|3(1) + 4(3)|}{\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} \sqrt{1^2 + 3^2}} \\ &= \frac{|3 + 12|}{\sqrt{9 + 16} \sqrt{1 + 9}} \\ &= \frac{15}{\sqrt{25} \sqrt{10}} \\ &= \frac{15}{5 \sqrt{10}} \\ &= \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}}$

