We are given the spherical coordinates $(\rho, \theta, \phi) = \left( 12, \frac{2 \pi}{3}, \frac{\pi}{4} \right)$. We want to convert these to rectangular coordinates $(x, y, z)$. The conversion formulas are
\begin{align*} x &= \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta \\ y &= \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta \\ z &= \rho \cos \phi \end{align*}
Plugging in the given values, we have
\begin{align*} x &= 12 \sin \frac{\pi}{4} \cos \frac{2 \pi}{3} = 12 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \cdot \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) = 12 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \cdot \left( -\frac{1}{2} \right) = -3 \sqrt{2} \\ y &= 12 \sin \frac{\pi}{4} \sin \frac{2 \pi}{3} = 12 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = 3 \sqrt{6} \\ z &= 12 \cos \frac{\pi}{4} = 12 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = 6 \sqrt{2} \end{align*}
We want to find $x+z$. We have $x+z = -3 \sqrt{2} + 6 \sqrt{2} = 3 \sqrt{2}$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{3\sqrt{2}}$

