Let the first line be $L_1$ and the second line be $L_2.$  Then $L_1$ is given by
\[ \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 + t \\ -3 - 7t \end{pmatrix}, \]and $L_2$ is given by
\[ \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} + s \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 + s \\ -5 - 7s \end{pmatrix}. \]The direction vector is $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix}.$  We can take the point $P = (2,-3)$ on $L_1$ and the point $Q = (1,-5)$ on $L_2.$  Then $\overrightarrow{PQ} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - 2 \\ -5 - (-3) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}.$  The distance between the lines is
\begin{align*} \frac{\left| \overrightarrow{PQ} \times \mathbf{v} \right|}{\|\mathbf{v}\|} &= \frac{\left| \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -2 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} \times \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} \right|}{\left\| \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \end{pmatrix} \right\|} \\ &= \frac{\left| \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ (-1)(-7) - (-2)(1) \end{pmatrix} \right|}{\sqrt{1^2 + (-7)^2}} \\ &= \frac{|7 + 2|}{\sqrt{1 + 49}} \\ &= \frac{9}{\sqrt{50}} \\ &= \frac{9}{5 \sqrt{2}} \\ &= \frac{9 \sqrt{2}}{5 \sqrt{2} \sqrt{2}} \\ &= \frac{9 \sqrt{2}}{10}. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\frac{9 \sqrt{2}}{10}}$

