Let $x$, 13, and 37 be the lengths of the sides of a non-degenerate triangle. By the triangle inequality, the sum of the lengths of any two sides must be greater than the length of the third side. Therefore, we have the following inequalities:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $x + 13 > 37 \Rightarrow x > 37 - 13 \Rightarrow x > 24$
    \item $x + 37 > 13 \Rightarrow x > 13 - 37 \Rightarrow x > -24$. Since $x$ is a side length, $x > 0$, so this inequality is always satisfied.
    \item $13 + 37 > x \Rightarrow 50 > x \Rightarrow x < 50$
\end{enumerate}
Combining the inequalities $x > 24$ and $x < 50$, we have $24 < x < 50$. Since $x$ is an integer, we have $25 \le x \le 49$. The number of integers in this range is $49 - 25 + 1 = 25$.

Thus, there are 25 possible integer values for $x$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{25}$

