Let $f(n) = \sum_{k=2}^\infty \frac{1}{k^n}.$ Then
\begin{align*} \sum_{n=2}^\infty f(n) &= \sum_{n=2}^\infty \sum_{k=2}^\infty \frac{1}{k^n} \\ &= \sum_{k=2}^\infty \sum_{n=2}^\infty \frac{1}{k^n} \\ &= \sum_{k=2}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{k^2} + \frac{1}{k^3} + \frac{1}{k^4} + \dotsb \right) \\ &= \sum_{k=2}^\infty \frac{1/k^2}{1 - 1/k} \\ &= \sum_{k=2}^\infty \frac{1/k^2}{(k - 1)/k} \\ &= \sum_{k=2}^\infty \frac{1}{k(k - 1)} \\ &= \sum_{k=2}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{k - 1} - \frac{1}{k} \right) \\ &= \left( \frac{1}{1} - \frac{1}{2} \right) + \left( \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{3} \right) + \left( \frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{4} \right) + \dotsb \\ &= 1. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{1}$

