Let $e^{i \alpha} = \frac{3}{5} + \frac{4}{5} i$. Then $\cos \alpha = \frac{3}{5}$ and $\sin \alpha = \frac{4}{5}$.
Let $e^{i \beta} = -\frac{12}{13} + \frac{5}{13} i$. Then $\cos \beta = -\frac{12}{13}$ and $\sin \beta = \frac{5}{13}$.
We want to find $\cos (\alpha - \beta)$. Using the cosine difference formula,
\begin{align*} \cos (\alpha - \beta) &= \cos \alpha \cos \beta + \sin \alpha \sin \beta \\ &= \left( \frac{3}{5} \right) \left( -\frac{12}{13} \right) + \left( \frac{4}{5} \right) \left( \frac{5}{13} \right) \\ &= -\frac{36}{65} + \frac{20}{65} \\ &= -\frac{16}{65} \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{-\frac{16}{65}}$

