Let the given determinant be
\[D = \begin{vmatrix} x & \sin \theta & \cos \theta \\ -\sin \theta & -x & 1 \\ \cos \theta & 1 & x \end{vmatrix}.\]Expanding along the first row, we have
\begin{align*} D &= x \begin{vmatrix} -x & 1 \\ 1 & x \end{vmatrix} - \sin \theta \begin{vmatrix} -\sin \theta & 1 \\ \cos \theta & x \end{vmatrix} + \cos \theta \begin{vmatrix} -\sin \theta & -x \\ \cos \theta & 1 \end{vmatrix} \\ &= x(-x^2 - 1) - \sin \theta (-\sin \theta x - \cos \theta) + \cos \theta (-\sin \theta - (-x) \cos \theta) \\ &= -x^3 - x + \sin^2 \theta x + \sin \theta \cos \theta - \sin \theta \cos \theta + x \cos^2 \theta \\ &= -x^3 - x + x (\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta) \\ &= -x^3 - x + x(1) \\ &= -x^3 - x + x \\ &= -x^3. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{-x^3}$

