Let $(r, \theta, z)$ be the cylindrical coordinates of a point. Then the rectangular coordinates $(x, y, z)$ are given by
\begin{align*} x &= r \cos \theta \\ y &= r \sin \theta \\ z &= z \end{align*}
In our case, we have $r = 6 \sqrt{3}$, $\theta = \frac{5 \pi}{3}$, and $z = -2$. Then
\begin{align*} x &= (6 \sqrt{3}) \cos \frac{5 \pi}{3} = (6 \sqrt{3}) \cos \left( 2 \pi - \frac{\pi}{3} \right) = (6 \sqrt{3}) \cos \frac{\pi}{3} = (6 \sqrt{3}) \left( \frac{1}{2} \right) = 3 \sqrt{3} \\ y &= (6 \sqrt{3}) \sin \frac{5 \pi}{3} = (6 \sqrt{3}) \sin \left( 2 \pi - \frac{\pi}{3} \right) = (6 \sqrt{3}) \left( -\sin \frac{\pi}{3} \right) = (6 \sqrt{3}) \left( -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \right) = -6 \cdot \frac{3}{2} = -9 \\ z &= -2 \end{align*}
Therefore, the rectangular coordinates are $(3 \sqrt{3}, -9, -2)$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{(3 \sqrt{3}, -9, -2)}$

