To solve the system of equations
\begin{align*} 5u &= -7 - 2v,\\ 3u &= 4v - 25 \end{align*} we can rewrite the equations as
\begin{align*} 5u + 2v &= -7,\\ 3u - 4v &= -25 \end{align*}
We can multiply the first equation by 2 to get $10u + 4v = -14$. Adding this to the second equation, we have
\begin{align*} (10u + 4v) + (3u - 4v) &= -14 + (-25) \\ 13u &= -39 \\ u &= -3 \end{align*}
Substituting $u = -3$ into the first equation $5u + 2v = -7$, we have
\begin{align*} 5(-3) + 2v &= -7 \\ -15 + 2v &= -7 \\ 2v &= -7 + 15 \\ 2v &= 8 \\ v &= 4 \end{align*}
Therefore, the solution is $(u,v) = (-3,4)$.
We can check our solution in the original equations:
\begin{align*} 5u &= 5(-3) = -15 \\ -7 - 2v &= -7 - 2(4) = -7 - 8 = -15 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} 3u &= 3(-3) = -9 \\ 4v - 25 &= 4(4) - 25 = 16 - 25 = -9 \end{align*}
Since both equations are satisfied, our solution is correct.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{(-3,4)}$

