We are given that $\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & 0 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{B}^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -1 & 3 \end{pmatrix}.$ We want to find $(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B})^{-1}.$
We know that $(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B})^{-1} = \mathbf{B}^{-1} \mathbf{A}^{-1}.$
Therefore,
\begin{align*} (\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B})^{-1} &= \mathbf{B}^{-1} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -1 & 3 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 4 & 0 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} (2)(4) + (-1)(1) & (2)(0) + (-1)(-1) \\ (-1)(4) + (3)(1) & (-1)(0) + (3)(-1) \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 8 - 1 & 0 + 1 \\ -4 + 3 & 0 - 3 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 1 \\ -1 & -3 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 1 \\ -1 & -3 \end{pmatrix}}$

