Let $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.$  Then the direction vector of the first line is $\mathbf{v}$ and the direction vector of the second line is $\mathbf{w}.$  We have
\begin{align*} \cos \theta &= \frac{|\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{w}|}{\|\mathbf{v}\| \|\mathbf{w}\|} \\ &= \frac{\left| \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \right|}{\left\| \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \right\| \left\| \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \right\|} \\ &= \frac{|(2)(-1) + (1)(1)|}{\sqrt{2^2 + 1^2} \sqrt{(-1)^2 + 1^2}} \\ &= \frac{|-2 + 1|}{\sqrt{4 + 1} \sqrt{1 + 1}} \\ &= \frac{|-1|}{\sqrt{5} \sqrt{2}} \\ &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\frac{1}{\sqrt{10}}}$

